 "method": "Primary source: DoD UFS 3-701-01 (2026) Table 4-1 MILCON area cost factors (e1, e2), the only free, current location index covering every state and the major metros. Metro factor = the most common ACF among DoD sites whose City field matches the metro's core city in that state (site counts and splits in dodDetail). Where no core-city site exists, adjacent cities stand in: VT-Burlington uses Colchester/Essex Junction. State factor = median ACF of all DoD sites in the state. That is a 'typical location in the state' value; for a project in a big metro, use the metro key (e.g. NY 1.03 vs NY-NewYorkCity 1.32, IL 1.02 vs IL-Chicago 1.21). Normalization: DoD sets the 96-base-city (two per CONUS state) average = 1.00. Check: the 2025 population-weighted mean of the state factors is 0.997, so values are used as published (no rescaling); US = 1.00. Cross-check vs RLB Q3 2026 (e4-e6, crossCheck): DoD and RLB agree within about 5% for Austin, Dallas, Miami and Seattle. RLB's two measures disagree with each other by up to 40% for some cities (Chicago, DC, Portland, Charlotte), so they are not used to override CONUS values. Exception: Hawaii and Alaska. DoD's noncontiguous factors (HI 2.03-2.60, AK 2.20-4.94) are far above both RLB measures for Honolulu (~1.35-1.41 on the DoD scale), so HI and AK are derived (see derived). Factors are rounded to 2 decimals like the source. A value in region R converts to national with value / factors[R]; a metro falls back to its state, then to US.",
